<section xml:id="mathematics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mathematics</title>

  <p>
    Here I'll list some cool math stuff I've done / am doing.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="pde">
    <title>Numerical Methods for PDE's</title>

    <p>
      Recreational research, primarily working through an extensive list of
      topics provided in
      <url href="https://www.ehu.eus/aitor/">Aitor Bergara's</url> course on
      <url href="https://www.ehu.eus/aitor/irakas/fin/main.html"> numerical methods for PDE's</url>.
      Done under
      <url href="https://iiitd.ac.in/subhashreem">Dr. Subhashree Mohapatra</url>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig_waves">
      <caption>Forward in time, centered in space</caption>
      <sidebyside margins="auto" valign="middle">
        <image source="advection_start">
          <description>
            <p>
              Start of advection
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>

        <image source="figure_ftcs">
          <description>
            <p>
              FTCS failing, we see more ridges as time goes on
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="stemvibe">
    <title>STEMvibe</title>

    <p>
      I've been involved with STEMvibe's national mathematics events for a while
      now.
      I've contributed problems for the 2025 Integral Cup finals in IIT Bombay
      and the 2026 Olympiad in IIT Delhi.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'll go ahead and list some of my favorite questions!
    </p>

    <subsubsection xml:id="integrals">
      <title>Integrals</title>

      <p>
        Some of my integrals for the grand finale at IIT Bombay:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <md number="yes">
                \int_{-1}^{1}\sum_{a=1}^{2025}\sum_{b=1}^{2025}\left\lfloor \frac{x}{a+b}-\frac{1}{2}-\left\lfloor \frac{x}{a+b} \right\rfloor \right\rfloor \sin(a\pi x)\sin(b\pi x)\,\mathrm{d}x
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              An intimidating question but once you've got the floor term
              figured out (which is just a binary 0/1 signal), it simplifies.
              This question was featured on the Pringles Math Instagram channel.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <md number="yes">
                \int_{0}^{5!} \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left\{\frac{x}{n!}\right\}\,\mathrm{d}x
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              A simple question which gets harder to deal with if we rewrite the
              fractional part into identity minus floor.
              Uses the periodicity of the fractional function.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <md number="yes">
                \int_{\pi}^{\infty}\frac{\log_{\pi}(e)}{\pi x^2}\frac{x\pi^{x/\pi}-\pi^2}{1-\log_{\pi}x}\,\mathrm{d}x
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              A complicated looking question which simplifies under the
              motivated <m>t=x/\pi</m> substitution.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <md number="yes">
                \int_{-1}^{1}\cfrac{1}{1-\cfrac{x}{1+x-\cfrac{x^2}{1+x^2-\cfrac{x^3}{1+x^3-\cdots\cfrac{x^{2025}}{1+x^{2025}}}}}}\,\mathrm{d}x
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              An un-yielding question under conventional integration tricks;
              this question rewards Olympiad-style thinking.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Silver, a calculus YouTuber also made videos covering questions from the
        entire competition on his channel.
        This competition also made national headlines as the "largest
        integration bee in the world".
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="olympiads">
      <title>Olympiads</title>

      <p>
        Some proof based questions I made:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define a sequence <m>\{a_k\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</m> with <m>a_0=1</m>
              and
              <md number="yes">
                a_n=\cfrac{1}{1-\cfrac{i/2}{1+i/2-\cfrac{i/2}{1+i/2-\underbrace{\dots}_{\textnormal{$n$ times}}\cfrac{i/2}{1+i/2}}}}
              </md>
              where <m>i=\sqrt{-1}</m>.
              Find the length of the path traced out by the points
              <m>a_0\rightarrow a_1 \rightarrow a_2 \rightarrow \cdots</m> on
              the complex plane.
            </p>

            <p>
              An unconventional use for continued fractions.
              This doesn't fall cleanly into recursion and can be solved
              geometrically after simplification very quickly.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Alice and Bob play a game starting with a running total
              <m>T = 0</m>.
              They take turns adding an integer <m>k</m> from the set
              <m>S = \{1, 2, 3, \dots, 15\}</m> to the total.
              Let <m>n \ge 2</m> be a fixed integer.
              The game terminates immediately when the total <m>T</m> becomes an
              <m>n</m>-digit number (i.e., when <m>T \ge 10^{n-1}</m> ).
              The player who makes the final move wins if the tens' digit of the
              final total is even, and loses if the tens' digit is odd.
              Assuming both players play optimally, determine for which values
              of $n$ Alice has a winning strategy, and for which values of $n$
              Bob has a winning strategy.
            </p>

            <p>
              A fun twist I made by adding a constraint on the tens' digit of a
              number.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Define two <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices:
              <md>
                L = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}, \quad R = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}.
              </md>
              We define a sequence of sets of vectors
              <m>\{S_k\}_{k=1}^{\infty}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

